subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I don't know which bank you mean, so check that fund's prospectus fee-table footnotes and SAI. Here are the general structures.\n\n**Unitary-fee arrangement**\n- The adviser takes one all-in fee and pays most fund expenses itself. Typical exclusions are brokerage, taxes, interest, acquired-fund fees and extraordinary costs.\n- Any waiver usually just reduces that single fee, often under a contractual term through a stated date.\n- Such waivers are typically not recoupable. Because the adviser already bears the expenses, the waived amount is simply forgone.\n\n**Ordinary expense-limitation agreement**\n- The adviser waives fees or reimburses expenses so that total operating expenses stay under a cap.\n- Agreements commonly allow recoupment within 36 months of the waiver.\n- Recoupment is usually limited to the lesser of the cap in effect at waiver and the current cap.\n- It often requires board approval, so future shareholders may bear past subsidies.\n\n**Bottom line:** unitary waivers are a permanent fee cut, while expense-limitation waivers are often a contingent deferral.","ttft_ms":2564,"type":"result","duration_ms":6437,"uuid":"1dc85318-d23c-495d-b3fd-d4217b43093e","ttft_stream_ms":597,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":598,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
